---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.6 (Day 2) — Dungeon Crawler RPG Build: Multi-Floor Campaign &amp; Boss</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.9, 2.11 · 90-Minute Design-First RPG Build (Day 2 of 2).</strong> Yesterday
    in <code>4.6a</code> you built the combat layer (<code>simulateDuelToDeath</code> and
    <code>runCombat</code>) inside <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>. Today you wrap that combat engine
    inside a <strong>floor exploration loop</strong> and an outer <strong>multi-floor campaign
    loop</strong>—demonstrating how clean helper methods prevent 3-deep tangled code.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (Continue in Your Existing Files):</strong>
    <p style="margin-top:0.35rem; margin-bottom:0.4rem;">
        Keep working in your <code>DungeonLoop.java</code> alongside
        <a href="../data/Dice.java" download>Dice.java</a>. (Need a fresh copy? Download
        <a href="../data/DungeonLoop.java" download>DungeonLoop.java</a>.)
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Answer the Guided Design questions on helper
            delegation, post-mortem healing guards, campfire placement, and clamping overheals.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; test in the sandbox:</strong> Draft each method below, click
            <strong>Copy</strong> to paste into <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>, and inspect Parts
            3–4 with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac Dice.java DungeonLoop.java
java DungeonLoop</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Full Suite):</strong> Unlock <code>DungeonLoopCheck.java</code>
        below and aim for <strong>13 passed, 0 failed</strong> across all 4 parts on your first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Single-Floor Room Loop + Helper Delegation</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~40 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>exploreFloorDeterministic(int startingHP, int numRooms, int monsterBaseHP, int playerDmg, int monsterDmg)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            A dungeon floor is a linear gauntlet of <code>numRooms</code> chambers where monsters grow
            tougher deeper down the hallway. Rather than copy-pasting the duel <code>while</code>
            loop from Day 1, <code>exploreFloorDeterministic</code> iterates room by room and
            <strong>calls <code>simulateDuelToDeath</code></strong> to resolve each room's battle.
        </p>
        <h4>Floor Rules, Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>If <code>startingHP &lt;= 0</code>, return <code>0</code> immediately.</li>
            <li>Walk rooms <code>r = 1</code> through <code>numRooms</code> inclusive:
                <ol>
                    <li>Compute monster HP: <code>monsterBaseHP + r * 2</code>.</li>
                    <li>Update <code>hp</code> by calling
                        <code>simulateDuelToDeath(hp, playerDmg, roomMonsterHP, monsterDmg)</code>.</li>
                    <li>If <code>hp &lt;= 0</code>, return <code>0</code> immediately!</li>
                    <li>If the hero <strong>survived</strong> room <code>r</code> AND
                        <code>r % 3 == 0</code>, a Healing Shrine restores <code>+4</code> HP.</li>
                </ol>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> remaining HP after room
                <code>numRooms</code>, or <code>0</code> if defeated earlier.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(20, 3, 4, 5, 3)</code> &rarr; R1 (mHP <code>6</code>): <code>17</code>; R2
                (mHP <code>8</code>): <code>14</code>; R3 (mHP <code>10</code>): <code>11</code> +
                <code>4</code> shrine = <code>15</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(8, 3, 4, 5, 3)</code> &rarr; R1: <code>5</code>; R2: <code>2</code>; R3:
                hero falls to <code>0</code> HP in the duel &rarr; returns <code>0</code> (must NOT
                receive Room 3's shrine heal!).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>exploreFloorDeterministic</code></h3>
        <p>Plan the loop type, helper call, and death-before-shrine order:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Loop Choice &amp; Modularity:</strong> Should the room traversal use a
                <code>for</code> loop or a <code>while</code> loop, and why? Why is calling
                <code>simulateDuelToDeath(...)</code> inside that loop better design than nesting
                another raw <code>while</code> loop directly inside
                <code>exploreFloorDeterministic</code>?</li>
            <li><strong>Order of Operations (Dead-Hero Shrine Trap):</strong> In
                <code>exploreFloorDeterministic(8, 3, 4, 5, 3)</code>, the hero dies in Room 3
                (which is divisible by <code>3</code>). What bug happens if you check
                <code>if (r % 3 == 0)</code> <em>before</em> checking
                <code>if (hp &lt;= 0) return 0;</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>exploreFloorDeterministic</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>, and check both Part 3 scenarios in
                <code>java DungeonLoop</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · Multi-Floor Nested Campaign</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>runMultiFloorCampaign(int startingHP, int maxHP, int floors, int roomsPerFloor, int playerDmg, int monsterDmg)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            The full campaign engine stacks three levels of control: an outer <code>for</code> loop
            over <code>floors</code>, an inner <code>for</code> loop over <code>roomsPerFloor</code>,
            and your <code>while</code>-loop duel inside <code>simulateDuelToDeath</code>. Returning
            <code>-floor</code> on defeat lets the campaign summary tell the player <em>exactly which
            floor claimed their hero</em>.
        </p>
        <h4>Campaign Rules, Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li>For each <code>floor = 1 .. floors</code>, clear each <code>room = 1 .. roomsPerFloor</code>:
                <ol>
                    <li>Monster HP scales with depth: <code>int monsterHP = floor * 4 + room * 2;</code>.</li>
                    <li>Update <code>hp = simulateDuelToDeath(hp, playerDmg, monsterHP, monsterDmg);</code>.</li>
                    <li>If <code>hp &lt;= 0</code>, immediately return <code>-floor</code>.</li>
                </ol>
            </li>
            <li><strong>End-of-Floor Campfire Rest:</strong> After clearing every room on a floor
                (between the end of the room loop and the end of the floor loop), restore
                <code>floor * 3</code> HP—<strong>clamped at <code>maxHP</code></strong>!</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>int</code>):</strong> final positive HP if all floors are
                cleared, or <code>-floor</code> where the hero died.</li>
        </ul>
        <h4>Walkthrough Examples</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>(25, 25, 2, 2, 6, 4)</code> &rarr; F1 clears at <code>21</code> + campfire
                <code>+3</code> = <code>24</code>; F2 clears at <code>16</code> + campfire
                <code>+6</code> = <code>22</code>.</li>
            <li><code>(25, 25, 1, 2, 20, 4)</code> &rarr; F1 clears at <code>25</code>; campfire
                <code>+3</code> is capped at <code>maxHP = 25</code> (returns <code>25</code>, not
                <code>28</code>!).</li>
            <li><code>(10, 25, 3, 2, 5, 4)</code> &rarr; survives F1 (<code>5 HP</code>), falls on
                F2 &rarr; returns <code>-2</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>runMultiFloorCampaign</code></h3>
        <p>Reason through brace placement, early exit, and clamping:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Brace Placement (Campfire Scope):</strong> Why must the campfire heal
                (<code>hp += floor * 3;</code>) sit <em>outside</em> the inner <code>room</code>
                loop yet <em>inside</em> the outer <code>floor</code> loop? How many times would a
                hero wrongly heal on a 3-floor, 4-room campaign if it were inside the room loop?</li>
            <li><strong>Clamping &amp; Early Return vs. <code>break</code>:</strong> Explain two
                bugs that occur if you forget (a) clamping <code>hp</code> against <code>maxHP</code>
                after the campfire, and (b) using <code>return -floor;</code> instead of
                <code>break;</code> when <code>hp &lt;= 0</code> inside the room loop?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>runMultiFloorCampaign</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonLoop.java</code>, and check the sandbox campaign outputs in
                <code>java DungeonLoop</code> before unlocking <code>DungeonLoopCheck.java</code>
                below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Day 2 Milestone — Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All four methods in <code>DungeonLoop.java</code> produce expected results in <code>java DungeonLoop</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>DungeonLoopCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>13 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> across all 4 parts on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/dungeon-crawl.html">Dungeon Crawl Extended (Student RPG Guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/nested-loops.html">Nested Loops</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
